import ColouringFormula

/-!
# The plane over `ℚ(√47)` needs four colours, if its colouring formula is unsatisfiable

**Theorem** (`Sqrt47.not_colorable_three_of_unsatisfiable`). If the colouring formula `q47.cnf` is
unsatisfiable, the unit-distance graph on `ℚ(√47)²` is not 3-colourable (`notes/quadratic_planes.md`); so
`χ(ℚ(√47)²) ≥ 4`. The upper bound `χ ≤ 5`, by reduction at 11, is not formalized here.
cake_lpr, a verified proof checker, checked an LRAT proof of the hypothesis
(`data/quadratic_planes/cake_lpr_checks.txt`); the proof is too large for Mathlib's `lrat_proof`. This file is
written from the data by `tools/field_lean.py`.

*Lower bound* (`not_colorable_three_of_unsatisfiable`). The graph of `data/quadratic_planes/q47.json` has 816
vertices `[a, b, c, e]`, standing for `((a + b√47)/240, (c + e√47)/240)`, and 2134 edges. The kernel checks that
each edge has length 1 (`checkEdges_E`, `QuadraticPlanes.adj_pt`). `formula` is the colouring formula of the graph,
`QuadraticPlanes.colourCNF E 0 687` (2448 variables, 7220 clauses), and a 3-colouring would satisfy it
(`QuadraticPlanes.not_colorable_of_unsatisfiable`). When the file is built, `#guard` checks that
`data/quadratic_planes/q47.cnf` (95 KB) is this formula.
-/

namespace Sqrt47

open LocalColouring QuadraticPlanes

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000 in
